<h2>Automating the entire workflow</h2>

<p>Everything described above can and should run on autopilot. Here is what the complete automated workflow looks like, from service completion to published review:</p>

<p><strong>1. Service marked complete.</strong> When you close out a job, mark an appointment as done, or complete a service in your CRM, the automation triggers. No manual action required beyond updating the job status, which you should be doing anyway.</p>

<p><strong>2. Satisfaction check sent.</strong> An SMS goes out within an hour of completion. Something like: "Hey [name], thanks for choosing us today. Quick question: how was your experience? [happy face / sad face]." SMS works best here because open rates are above 90%, compared to roughly 20% for email.</p>

<p><strong>3. Happy path or recovery path.</strong> If they tap the happy option, they land on your Google review page (or whichever platform is next in your rotation). If they tap the unhappy option, they land on a private feedback form. You get notified immediately so you can follow up personally.</p>

<p><strong>4. Follow-up for non-responders.</strong> If someone does not respond to the initial SMS within two days, an <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email follow-up</a> goes out with a slightly different message. "No pressure at all. If you have a minute, we would really appreciate a quick review. Here is the link." One follow-up is enough. Two messages total, then stop.</p>

<p><strong>5. Review monitoring.</strong> New reviews across Google, Facebook, and other platforms are pulled into your dashboard. You get notified when a new review appears so you can respond promptly.</p>

<p><strong>6. Review embedding.</strong> Positive reviews are automatically pulled onto your website, keeping your social proof fresh without any manual updating.</p>

<p>This entire sequence runs in the background. Once it is set up, your only ongoing task is responding to reviews as they come in. Everything else, the asking, the filtering, the follow-up, the monitoring, happens automatically.</p>

<p>Building a <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">customer follow-up system</a> that includes review requests means you never have to remember to ask. Every happy customer gets an invitation to share their experience, every time, without fail.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Measuring what is working</h2>

<p>Like any system, your review automation should be tracked and refined over time. The numbers that matter are:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Request-to-review conversion rate.</strong> What percentage of review requests actually result in a published review? A rate between 10-20% is typical. If you are below 10%, your timing or messaging needs work.</li>
  <li><strong>Response rate on satisfaction checks.</strong> If people are not even responding to your initial SMS, the message might be too long, too generic, or arriving at the wrong time.</li>
  <li><strong>Review velocity.</strong> How many new reviews are you getting per month? Consistency matters more than bursts. Five reviews a week beats fifty in one month followed by silence.</li>
  <li><strong>Average rating trend.</strong> Is your average rating stable, climbing, or slipping? A downward trend signals a service quality issue that no amount of automation can fix.</li>
  <li><strong>Platform distribution.</strong> Are your reviews concentrated on one platform, or are they spread across the sites that matter for your industry?</li>
</ul>

<p>Check these numbers monthly. Small adjustments to your timing, your message wording, or your platform rotation can compound into significantly better results over a quarter.</p>

